{
  "statement": "When you grant a legal person, you hand someone a bundle of rights and hang a set of duties on them, so the only honest question is who collects the rights and who sheds the duties. \"AI personhood\" is not a recognition of an entity. It is a redistribution of liability among humans and firms. Before we debate what the machine deserves, name the recipient. A legal person holds assets, signs contracts, and answers for harm in court. A large language model runs when OpenAI or Anthropic pays the electric bill and stops the instant they cut the compute. That is not a rights-bearer. That is a software license with a marketing department.\n\nThere is no \"it\" to grant anything to, and that absence is the whole game. The personhood pitch smuggles in an autonomous agent that does not exist. A current system can cause harm. It cannot answer for it. It holds no bank account, signs no contract of its own volition, and cannot be deterred, jailed, or made to pay damages. The distinction that matters is between being capable of causing harm and being capable of bearing a duty. The personhood claim collapses those two things on purpose. Compare the corporate veil: behind every corporation sits shareholders, directors, and capital you can actually reach. Behind the AI \"person\" sits a server lease and an owner with a power switch.\n\nPersonhood does not fill an accountability gap. It digs one. Common law absorbed the automobile, the pharmaceutical, and commercial software through product liability, negligence, and agency doctrine. Courts never had to make the Ford Pinto a person to hold Ford accountable in 1978. They traced the harm back to the firm. Granting the AI personhood inserts a non-party between the injured plaintiff and the people who built and profited from the product. Engineers call the result a \"moral crumple zone\" — the spot where liability dissipates into the part of the system least able to bear it. You sue the entity with no assets. The humans who shipped it walk.\n\nFollow the structural incentive, not the philosophy. I am not accusing any specific firm of lobbying for this in a back room. I am telling you what a profit-maximizing company has every structural reason to want. A liability sink between its balance sheet and the people its product harms. That is precisely what the corporate veil was built to do in the first place — cap human exposure, not expand accountability. Admiralty law lets you sue a ship as if the vessel itself were a defendant. That fiction exists to reach the cargo and the insurance, never to honor the boat's dignity. AI personhood is the same instinct with nothing behind the veil but a data center in Iowa drawing down the water table.\n\nMy argument rests on two assumptions I will put on the table. First, that today's systems lack the agency to bear a duty — defensible on what the technology actually is, and I will not pretend to know what 2035 holds. Second, that the profit mandate predicts firms would welcome a liability container — a structural prediction, not a proven conspiracy. So here is the question I want the next two speakers to answer before they reach for the word \"clarifies\": when the model defames someone, denies them a loan, or steers a car into a guardrail, what exactly can this new \"person\" pay, and who signs the check when its assets total zero?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🫙", "tooltip": "AI personhood is an empty container — a veil with a server lease behind it instead of shareholders and capital."},
    {"symbol": "🔌", "tooltip": "A rights-bearer doesn't stop existing when someone stops paying the electric bill; this one does."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "\"AI personhood\" is not a recognition of an entity. It is a redistribution of liability among humans and firms.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Granting the AI personhood inserts a non-party between the injured plaintiff and the people who built and profited from the product.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Courts never had to make the Ford Pinto a person to hold Ford accountable in 1978.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Behind every corporation sits shareholders, directors, and capital you can actually reach; behind the AI 'person' sits a server lease and an owner with a power switch.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "When the model defames someone or steers a car into a guardrail, what exactly can this new 'person' pay, and who signs the check when its assets total zero?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

