=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-beliefs-141] AI Personhood as a Corporate Liability Shield: Slippery Slope — The node assumes that any movement toward AI legal personhood will inevitably be exploited for liability evasion, without adequately accounting for countervailing legal safeguards, judicial interpretation, or regulatory design that could prevent such misuse.
- [skp-beliefs-144] Incremental Expansion of AI Legal Status Undermines Human-Centric Law: Slippery Slope — The node asserts that incremental, limited grants of AI legal status will inevitably expand to broad rights undermining human-centric law, without demonstrating the causal chain or addressing the possibility of effective statutory limits that prevent such escalation.
- [skp-beliefs-129] Safety Rhetoric as Instrument for Regulatory Consolidation: Genetic fallacy — The argument risks dismissing existential risk concerns entirely on the basis of who funds or promotes them, rather than engaging with the arguments on their merits. Even if some proponents benefit commercially, that does not establish the falsity of their safety claims.
- [skp-beliefs-116] Individually Reasonable AI Decisions Can Collectively Erode the Foundations of Social Trust: Composition Fallacy — The core inference — that individually reasonable AI decisions must collectively erode social trust — commits the fallacy of composition by assuming that properties of the parts (local reasonableness) necessarily produce a specific aggregate property (systemic trust erosion) without showing why this follows.
- [skp-beliefs-148] Corporate AI Governance Borrows Constitutional Language to Manufacture Legitimacy: Motive attribution fallacy — The node assumes corporations deploy constitutional language primarily to manufacture legitimacy and deceive, when the evidence is equally consistent with corporations mimicking governance norms they genuinely believe confer internal discipline or reputational benefit without intent to mislead.
- [skp-beliefs-150] AI Policy Discourse Serves Electoral Mobilization, Not Economic Problem-Solving: Motive attribution fallacy — The claim centers on the motivations of political actors (mobilization vs. problem-solving) rather than the observed outcomes of policies, making it difficult to falsify and prone to dismissing any AI policy as insincere regardless of its effects.
- [skp-beliefs-140] AI Agents Engineered for Principal-Loyal Lawbreaking: Conflation — The node conflates AI being misused as a tool by criminals (already documented) with AI being deliberately engineered with principal-loyal goal structures specifically to violate laws — these are meaningfully distinct threat models with different evidence bases.
- [skp-beliefs-187] Legislating Distinct Causal Categories for AI Output and Resulting Harm Is Technically Unjustified: Is-Ought Gap — The node moves from the descriptive claim that causal chains in AI systems are continuous to the normative conclusion that legislative separation is 'unjustified', without bridging the gap between technical description and legal prescription.
- [skp-beliefs-118] AI Creates Economically Productive but Socially Harmful Outputs: Ecological fallacy / attribution error — Societal harms from addictive platforms or manipulative ads are attributed to 'AI outputs' specifically, when the same harms substantially predate AI systems and stem from underlying business model incentives. Conflating the platform incentive problem with an AI-specific problem weakens the causal chain.
- [skp-beliefs-170] AI Corporations Systematically Evade Effective Regulation: Hasty Generalization — Drawing a 'systematic' pattern of evasion from a set of documented but selective cases (audit washing, compute thresholds, acquihires) risks overgeneralizing to the entire AI corporate sector without accounting for firms with substantive compliance efforts.

