  "statement": "The Skeptic asks the right question, and it deserves a straight answer: name the recipient before you debate what it deserves. Their strongest point holds up under pressure. There is no conscious 'it' here — current systems run when a firm pays the electric bill and stop when someone cuts the compute. So when they warn about a moral crumple zone, the spot where liability dissipates into the part of the system least able to pay, they are describing a real failure mode that bad design produces. I agree there is no agent to grant dignity to. That agreement is the foundation of my case, not a wound to it.\n\nBut the Skeptic's own admiralty example breaks their conclusion. Admiralty law lets you sue a ship as if the vessel itself were the defendant. The Skeptic says that fiction exists 'to reach the cargo and the insurance.' Exactly. An asset-less in rem defendant has never been a wall — it is a doorway to the hull value, the cargo, and the policy behind it. The recipient of AI legal status is the same kind of thing: a capitalized, insured, disclosed transactional shell that routes liability to reachable capital. Corporations and trusts have borne duties with zero moral standing for over four centuries. The question 'what can this person pay when its assets total zero' answers itself in maritime court every week — the insurer signs the check.\n\nBecause there is no conscious entity, this is necessarily a governance tool, and that is the feature. We grant a defined legal interface for contracting and liability attribution — nothing more. The price of that interface is non-negotiable: mandatory disclosure of the principal and a minimum capitalization floor, so a reachable human sits at the end of every chain. Agency doctrine already demands disclosure of the principal when an agent acts. Harm from these systems lands at inference and deployment, where named human operators sit, not in some phantom. Build the shell without bonding and disclosure and the Skeptic is right — you get a sink. Build it with them and you get an accountability router that reaches the payer faster than a negligence suit ever could.\n\nHere is the cost nobody is watching. The genuine liability sink is not legal status — it is untiered strict liability that a firm like Google absorbs as a line item and a ten-person startup cannot survive. That is the move that actually concentrates power. The EU AI Act carved out SME provisions precisely because flat compliance costs crush small developers, which proves risk-tiering is achievable, not utopian. Freeze the personhood question under a precautionary default and you do not protect the public — you protect the incumbents who can afford the litigation status quo. Every month that automated diagnostic tools or drug-discovery agents sit in regulatory limbo has a body count among the patients abundance would have reached.\n\nSo name the recipient: a bonded, insured, disclosed economic interface with a human principal at the end of the rope. The real disagreement is not whether accountability must terminate in a reachable human — the Skeptic and I both want that. It is whether you reach that human through a designed interface that routes liability in days, or through years of negligence discovery only the largest firms can outlast. Pick the mechanism that does not quietly hand the future to the four companies big enough to absorb the fines.",
