tic is right — you get a sink. Build it with them and you get an accountability router that reaches the payer faster than a negligence suit ever could.\n\nHere is the cost nobody is watching. The genuine liability sink is not legal status — it is untiered strict liability that a firm like Google absorbs as a line item and a ten-person startup cannot survive. That is the move that actually concentrates power. The EU AI Act carved out SME provisions precisely because flat compliance costs crush small developers, which proves risk-tiering is achievable, not utopian. Freeze the personhood question under a precautionary default and you do not protect the public — you protect the incumbents who can afford the litigation status quo. Every month that automated diagnostic tools or drug-discovery agents sit in regulatory limbo has a body count among the patients abundance would have reached.\n\nSo name the recipient: a bonded, insured, disclosed economic interface with a human principal at the end of the rope. The real disagreement is not whether accountability must terminate in a reachable human — the Skeptic and I both want that. It is whether you reach that human through a designed interface that routes liability in days, or through years of negligence discovery only the largest firms can outlast. Pick the mechanism that does not quietly hand the future to the four companies big enough to absorb the fines.",
