  "statement": "The Skeptic is right that there is no conscious 'it' to receive rights, and that 'AI personhood' redistributes liability among humans and firms rather than recognizing an entity. That insight cuts through decades of philosophical fog. The Accelerationist is right that a bonded, insured, disclosed interface — an in-rem admiralty mechanism that lets the insurer sign the check — reaches a solvent payer faster than negligence discovery ever could. Both positions are correct, and both share one blind spot: they treat legal personhood as a question of who pays after the harm. The decisive question is whether the legal status preserves a human duty of care before the harm occurs. A regime that locates a payer and silently lowers the standard a human owes to third parties has found solvency and lost accountability.\n\nLegal-status design is upstream of harm, not downstream of it. Established negligence and fiduciary doctrine already holds a deployer to an objective standard of care — the prudent professional, the respondeat superior employer answering for the agent. The EU AI Liability Directive and the AI Act extend those deployer duties to AI-mediated conduct rather than displacing them; that is the correct architectural instinct. The danger is subtler than the moral crumple zone the Skeptic named. It is the silent erosion where a doctor, an underwriter, or a lender substitutes an AI system and the operative duty quietly drops from 'what a competent professional would do' to 'what the tool output.' For both rival positions to hold, automation would have to leave the human's standard of care untouched. It does not. The test of any status is whether the duty survives delegation — not whether a check clears.\n\nThe insurance point reaches a solvent balance sheet, and I will not pretend otherwise. A capitalization floor answers the solvency question; an insurer answers the payment question. Neither answers the deterrence question. Tort regimes are backward-looking and compensatory by design. They price what they can litigate. They cannot deter a diffuse, attenuated harm whose expected damages exceed any bond and whose causal chain is too remote to trace to one defendant. The Accelerationist's interface routes compensation well; it cannot deter what it cannot price. Finding a payer is not achieving safety — that conflation of legality with safety is precisely the error a serious regime must foreclose. Ask the deployer this directly: what deters a harm whose damages exceed the bond and whose chain is too attenuated to litigate?\n\nMandatory disclosure of the principal belongs in any regime as a floor — a human must be identifiable at every interaction, so no malicious actor hides behind a synthetic agent. Agency doctrine already requires it. But the Accelerationist's clean image of one human at the end of the rope assumes away the hard case. In layered, multi-agent deployments — an orchestrator calling sub-agents on a base model the operator never built and cannot audit — a single disclosed principal misleads more than it clarifies. The shield for upstream developers is not an inevitability; it is a design choice regulators can foreclose. The regime must specify whose duty governs when the chain is layered, and the recourse must reach the design choice where the consequential trade-off was actually made — not terminate at the ten-person operator who can least inspect the foundation model.\n\nOn tiering, the Accelerationist names a real harm: untiered strict liability that Google absorbs as a line item and a ten-person startup cannot survive does concentrate power among the four largest firms. Compliance cost should scale with firm size. But there is a line. Tiering the administrative burden is fairness; tiering the duty owed to third parties is a deterrence gap dressed as a safe harbor. A patient injured by a startup's diagnostic model is owed the same standard of care as one injured by a giant's. Proportionate burden, undiminished duty. We spent two centuries building the duty of care that lets a stranger trust a professional they never met. The question before this committee is not whether the check clears. It is whether that duty survives the machine.",
