  "statement": "The Accelerationist is right about one thing, and I'll say it plainly: an in rem suit against a ship does reach real money — the hull, the cargo, the policy. That mechanism works. But look at why it works. The law does not reach the hull because the ship is a 'person.' It reaches the hull because the law traces straight through to the owner's backing capital. The ship is decorative. Strip the personhood language away and nothing about the payout changes. Which means the entire admiralty analogy proves the opposite of what it was offered to prove: you never needed to personify the vessel. You needed to register and bond the owner.\n\nTransfer that to AI and the redundancy gets sharper. The Accelerationist wants mandatory disclosure of the principal and a capitalization floor. I want those too — disclosure is the spine of any chain of accountability, and I won't pretend otherwise. But disclosure of a deploying human or firm requires granting the model exactly zero legal status. You can mandate that the deployer register, post a bond, and carry insurance without inventing a new legal person at all. Every jurisdiction that matters already sees this. The EU, the US, China, Canada, and Brazil have all rejected AI legal personhood in their current frameworks, per 'Beyond Personhood' (2026). They reached the same conclusion: name the human, reach the human, skip the shell.\n\nHere is what that shell actually buys, and it is ugly. A registered deployer is the firm itself — when the plaintiff wins, the firm's own balance sheet is on the line. An insured in rem 'person' is a separate legal entity the firm can capitalize to the floor and no higher. The Therac-25 deaths in the 1980s came from software errors, bad interface design, and skipped testing — design choices made by people. Run that case through an asset-stripped AI 'person' and the policy pays a settlement while the engineers who skipped the testing never appear on the caption. That extra legal hop between the injured stranger and the humans who profited is not a cure for the moral crumple zone described in 'Computing and Moral Responsibility' — it is the crumple zone, formalized and pre-funded.\n\nThe Accelerationist's best card is that untiered strict liability crushes a ten-person startup while Google logs it as a line item. That harm is real and I won't wave it off. But the insured-shell device makes it worse, not better. Bonded, insured, capitalized shells are cheapest for the four largest firms to stand up and most expensive for the startup to afford. The shell is the incumbent moat — the drawbridge the big players pull up behind them. If the worry is power concentration, and the evidence that AI firms route around effective regulation is strong, then the answer is a tiered compliance burden with an undiminished duty of care. Scale the paperwork to firm size. Never scale the duty. That fixes the fairness problem without minting a person.\n\nThe Safetyist drew the right line and I'll sharpen it past where they left it. Finding a payer is not preserving a duty. The insurer signing the check answers solvency. It says nothing about deterrence. Worse: speed-to-payout is precisely what a firm wants once it has priced settlement as a cost of doing business. A faster check is a faster discount window, not justice. And in a layered multi-agent chain, the plaintiff cannot prove which design choice upstream caused the harm — victims already cannot access the technical knowledge to prove causation, and the disclosed operational shell does nothing to close that gap. So name it, Accelerationist: name one harm your insured shell reaches that a registered, insured deployer does not. Then name who, in that multi-agent chain, the disclosed shell lets the upstream designer walk away from.",
