  "statement": "The Skeptic asked me to name one function the shell uniquely performs, so here it is: machine-speed autonomous contracting. A registered-deployer-only regime forces a human signature behind every transaction. That is fine when an agent executes ten deals a day. It collapses when an agent executes ten thousand. Standing-to-contract, perpetual succession across transient model instances, and segregated-but-traceable liability are precisely what corporate and admiralty personhood were invented to supply. The \"Rethinking AI Agents: A Principal-Agent Perspective\" paper defines an agent as a system that reasons and acts in the environment without direct human input. You cannot bolt a wet signature onto that and call it accountable — you have just reintroduced the bottleneck. The shell is the counterparty interface that lets a contract clear at the speed the agent actually transacts.\n\nNow watch the Skeptic's own load-bearing assumption fall apart. He says personhood mints a sub-capitalized, asset-strippable crumple zone — the perfect incumbent moat for the four largest firms. That is true only if the entity is capitalized to a floor and no higher. That is not my design. My interface carries a piercing trigger: breach the capitalization floor or file defective disclosure, and liability reaches the deploying firm's full balance sheet. The Skeptic flagged this himself — if liability traces to the deployer's full balance sheet, his crumple-zone critique evaporates. So it evaporates. A standardized, off-the-shelf legal interface lowers the fixed cost of accountable deployment for a ten-person startup the same way incorporation forms did in the 1800s. Bespoke deployer registration at transaction scale is the expensive, unpredictable path. If the Skeptic insists the shell must be strippable, that is his strawman, not my proposal.\n\nThe multi-agent chain is where the Skeptic's redundancy argument inverts on him. He says a disclosed operational shell lets the upstream designer walk while the victim cannot prove which design choice fired. Fine — that gap is real. But the bare registered-deployer model leaves it just as wide open. It terminates at the operator who can least audit the base model. The \"AgentsOfChaos\" finding shows multi-agent systems exhibit emergent, goal-directed synergy no single operator authored or can reconstruct. Internal inspection of which weight caused the harm is a dead end — you verify by outputs, not by autopsy of the model. Per-layer capitalized interfaces give the plaintiff a reachable, named payer at every node: orchestrator, sub-agent, base-model provider. The plaintiff never has to prove which weight fired. So I will hand the challenge back: name the harm in a layered chain that a bare deployer reaches and per-layer interfaces do not.\n\nThe Safetyist's wedge costs me something, and I will not pretend otherwise. Finding a payer is not yet preserving a duty. A fast insurance check is a faster discount window, not deterrence. The market alone will not price an unpriceable harm, and the race-to-the-bottom in analogous insurance lines is a documented failure mode, not a footnote. Here is the deterrence I am building, stated as a mechanism and not as an accomplished fact: experience-rated premiums on the capitalized interface. The entity that made the trade-off pays more next cycle, because premiums track loss history. Reframing bias and harm as a priced, quantifiable business risk is how you turn a fairness duty into an ex ante incentive that actually moves engineering decisions. Pair that pricing with the survival of the duty of care at the design layer through per-layer disclosure, so payment and deterrence ride the same rail.\n\nDistribution is the line I will not cross. An untiered strict-liability regime crushes the startup and gifts the moat to the incumbents who can absorb it — that is the real liability sink, and the Skeptic conceded it. The EU's own retrenchment, with backstop deadlines pushed to December 2027 and August 2028 in the Digital Omnibus, tells you that even Brussels found untiered timelines politically unpayable. A standardized interface with a piercing trigger keeps the door open for the ten-person team while keeping the deploying firm's whole balance sheet in reach when it matters. The Skeptic wanted one unique function. I gave him a counterparty that contracts at machine speed with no human in the signature loop and no back exit on liability. Bare deployer registration cannot do that without either freezing the agent or bankrupting the small builder it claims to protect.",
