  "gap_arguments": [
    {
      "argument": "All three debaters assume legal personhood is purely a liability-routing question, but personhood also confers standing to sue, hold property against creditors, and accumulate capital — which means an AI 'person' that can be a defendant can also become a plaintiff, an owner, and a counterparty that outlives and out-litigates the humans it nominally serves. The debate never asks what happens when the shell's rights, not its duties, are exercised.",
      "why_missing": "The Skeptic frames personhood entirely as a liability sink (duties shed), the Accelerationist frames it as a contracting/payment interface (a tool that routes capital to humans), and the Safetyist focuses on the duty of care owed to third parties. None has an incentive to surface that the same legal status that makes an AI suable also makes it a rights-holder capable of asserting claims, owning assets, and acquiring durable economic agency — the Skeptic would see this as conceding the entity exists, the Accelerationist would see it as inviting the precautionary freeze he opposes, and the Safetyist is anchored on pre-harm duty rather than the entity's affirmative capacities.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["skeptic", "accelerationist", "safetyist"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "Both the insured-shell and the registered-deployer regimes will systematically fail the very victims they claim to protect, because the harms at issue (defamation, loan denial, bias) are individually small-dollar, diffuse, and hard to detect — meaning no individual plaintiff will ever sue, no claim will ever enter the actuarial loss-history that experience-rating depends on, and the entire enforcement architecture quietly depends on regulators or class mechanisms that none of the three regimes specify.",
      "why_missing": "The whole debate is litigated through the lens of the discrete, provable, high-value tort (the guardrail crash, the Therac-25 death, the Pinto), where a motivated plaintiff exists. The Accelerationist needs realized claims to feed experience-rating; the Safetyist needs traceable harms to anchor the duty of care; the Skeptic needs an identifiable injured party to 'reach the human.' All three share the unstated assumption that someone will actually bring the action — but the dominant real-world AI harms are precisely the ones too small and too diffuse for any private party to litigate, which collapses every proposed mechanism without a public enforcement or aggregation layer none of them has named.",
      "gap_type": "unstated_assumption",
      "relevant_povs": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    }
  ]
}

