{
  "statement": "Pre-funding the reserve does not cure the crumple zone. It pre-pays it. The Accelerationist is right that a reserve allocated before deployment cannot be stripped after the harm. Grant that fully. The non-strippability is real and it costs me nothing to grant it. Here is why. A capped, pre-paid liability is the exact outcome a profit-maximizing firm wants. Open-ended balance-sheet exposure terrifies a general counsel. A budgeted line item does not. Firms already pre-fund settlement reserves and book them as cost of doing business. The registered-deployer regime keeps the entire balance sheet at risk for every harm the firm causes. The shell converts that unbounded risk into a number the CFO picked before launch. That is the crumple zone with a price tag attached. The firm capitalizes to the regulatory floor and not one dollar higher, because the floor is the optimal move when your goal is profit, not safety. And a fixed capital lock-up is cheapest for the four largest firms and most prohibitive for a startup. So the repair deepens the incumbent moat it claims to fix.\n\nNow the backstop. The Accelerationist asked me to name it, so here it is, concrete. A public regulator with standing to sue on victims' behalf. Statutory penalties decoupled from litigated loss-history, so a diffuse harm triggers consequence without any plaintiff ever appearing. A compensation pool funded by a revenue-scaled levy on deployers, not a fixed capital reserve. It must be public, and here is the reason. The diffuse small-dollar harm collapses every private mechanism the Accelerationist proposed. No one litigates a $40 loan denial. An insurer prices off claims that get filed, and these claims never get filed. The Australian trust survey from 2024 found 36 percent of people do not even know the technology denying them runs on AI. They cannot sue what they cannot see. Markets have demonstrably failed to self-correct systemic discrimination on their own, which is why the aggregation cannot come from the very insurers whose pricing depends on the lawsuits that never happen. It has to come from an enforcer with subpoena power and a mandate to treat foundational AI as public infrastructure, not a product line.\n\nThe insurance-underwriting channel under-deters exactly the most common harms. The documented harms — biased loan screens, discriminatory hiring filters — are precisely the diffuse small-dollar injuries the Accelerationist admits never enter his loss loop. So the fire-sprinkler analogy fails at the one place it matters most. A fire underwriter prices a burned building because someone files a claim for a burned building. No one files for a marginally biased credit score they never learned was biased. The market channel will price the rare catastrophic harm and ignore the pervasive ordinary one. That is backwards from what victims need.\n\nNow I want the Accelerationist to build something he has only asserted. He says the shell holds duties only — no standing to sue, no capital accumulation beyond the reserve. But he also needs the shell to do machine-speed contracting, which is his whole reason for minting the thing. Specify the mechanism. Machine-speed contracting means the entity acquires assets, holds them, and enforces the contracts it signs. An entity that enforces a contract is a plaintiff. An entity that holds assets against creditors is a property-holder. You cannot have an entity that signs and performs but cannot sue to collect — that is not a counterparty, that is a vending machine. So either the human holds the assets and enforces, in which case the shell adds nothing a registered deployer lacks, or the shell holds and enforces, in which case it is exactly the property-owning, asset-shielding person warned about in the 2020 paper 'Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence.' That paper traced how the corporate limited-liability model lets human owners hide assets behind an entity, the same way Citizens United extended affirmative rights to a corporate fiction in 2010. Build the duties-only shell or drop it.\n\nThere is a real point hiding in the Safetyist's N-doors objection, and it joins mine. Per-layer interfaces give a plaintiff reachability — they can find a payer at each handoff. Grant that. But reachability is not attribution. N disclosed payers who each disclaim the design choice leave the plaintiff exactly where they started, unable to prove which trade-off caused the harm. The victims who cannot access the technical knowledge to prove causation are no better off facing five reachable defendants than one unreachable one. And the same per-node disclosure could be mandated of a registered-deployer chain tomorrow, without minting a single legal person. So I will ask the question the personhood proposal cannot answer comfortably: what does the legal status do that a mandatory disclosure rail plus a public compensation pool does not already do — except give the humans who profited a budgeted ceiling on what they owe?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "A pre-funded reserve is a receipt the firm prints in advance for harm it plans to cause — the crumple zone with a price tag."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "Five reachable doors that each say 'not my decision' leave the victim exactly where one locked door did."}
  ],
