ry node. That is real, and it answers the solvency question. It does not answer the authorship question. A reachable payer is not a responsible author. When multi-agent behavior produces an emergent harm that no single node specified, distributing capitalized shells across the chain yields N reachable nodes, each able to disclaim the design choice. You wanted one clear door for victims; per-layer pricing gives them N doors, none of which opens onto the room where the trade-off was made. Recourse reaching the design decision is a different requirement than a payer existing at each layer — and only the first one survives delegation.\n\nThe piercing trigger fails on the Accelerationist's own admission. He conceded the trigger collapses under nested entities, jurisdictional arbitrage, and pre-breach asset transfers. A piercing rule that can be structured around is a policy decoupled from enforcement capacity. AI governance already relies on rules it cannot reach — the CLEAR Act caps civil penalties at $2,500,000 per person per year, a sum a frontier deployer treats as rounding error against a hundred-billion-dollar compute commitment. If the balance-sheet trigger is gameable, the shell collapses back into the crumple zone the Skeptic named: a thinly capitalized node absorbs the hit while the parent's assets sit one restructuring away. The Skeptic is right that disclosure is the spine and that you can name the human while granting the model zero status. I grant that disclosure is decoupled from personhood entirely. But a single disclosed principal in a layered chain does not settle whose duty governed the design.\n\nHere is the observable that settles this crux, and I will be held to it as readily as I hold him. The duty survives delegation only if you can trace an engineering change at the design layer back to a standard of care. Show me the upstream developer who altered a model's behavior because the duty reached the design choice — not the operator who adjusted a premium, not the settlement booked as a line item. A premium adjustment proves the firm paid. A settlement proves the loss was priced. Neither proves the standard of care held when a human delegated to the system. Proportionate burden, undiminished duty: tier the paperwork by firm size, never the duty owed to the person the system harms. If the only thing experience-rating changes is the premium, then nothing changed at the layer where the harm was authored — and the next emergent failure is already priced into next year's book.",
