  "statement": "Experience-rating prices the past, and Safetyist is right that it cannot see an emergent tail with zero claims behind it. I am not defending claim-history pricing as the deterrent against the unmodeled catastrophe. That half of the critique lands. But catastrophe insurance has never priced the tail off claim history. Fire underwriters do not wait for a building to burn before they price it. They mandate sprinklers, fire doors, and inspection schedules as conditions of coverage. Cyber underwriters since roughly 2021 have required multi-factor authentication and segregated backups before they write a policy at all. That is the forward-looking proxy: insurers price the un-enumerable harm by conditioning coverage on observable engineering controls and reserves held against unmodeled loss. Govern AI risk through that private-market channel, and the underwriting condition reaches the design table before any plaintiff exists.\n\nThe duty of care must sit on top of the priced interface, and Safetyist's instinct here is correct, not redundant. Tier the paperwork by firm size. Never tier the duty owed to a third party. A startup files less; it does not owe less to the person it harms. This is not a pre-deployment gate. The Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act runs its sandbox for 36 months with a 60-day window to cure violations before the attorney general acts — that is the model of a duty that bites after deployment, not a permission slip required before it. I would convert the duty into a per-node disclosure obligation: each layer discloses the design trade-off it made, verifiable by its outputs. That opens the door to the room where the consequential choice was made. Deployment still proceeds. The duty still bites.\n\nNow the Skeptic's challenge — name one harm my per-layer interface reaches that a registered, insured deployer does not. Here it is: the emergent multi-agent failure no single operator authored. A terminal deployer cannot reconstruct which weight in which upstream model produced the harm. Black-box models defeat internal inspection, so weight-autopsy is a dead end for the plaintiff. Output-based verification at each node is the only path that works. Per-layer interfaces let a plaintiff prove harm by the observable output at each handoff, without proving the internal mechanism that no one can reconstruct. A single insured deployer regime terminates liability at the operator who can least audit the base model. That is the harm a one-door regime structurally cannot reach.\n\nThere is a shared blind spot both rival positions and I have left unnamed. Diffuse small-dollar harms — defamation, a wrongful loan denial, a biased screen — never enter the loss-history loop. No individual plaintiff sues over a $400 injury, so no claim feeds the pricing engine, and the deterrence quietly evaporates. The answer is an aggregation rail: mandatory standardized incident reporting and telemetry that feeds both the pricing engine and the duty standard. That reporting rail is the line-item answer. A premium passed through as a cost does nothing; a reported pattern of denials that raises the underwriting condition and triggers the duty does. The reporting makes the diffuse harm visible so it can be priced and deterred at the design choice, not absorbed by Google as a rounding error.\n\nOn the piercing trigger I conceded was gameable — pre-funded segregated reserves allocated before deployment make the reserve non-strippable, and pre-committed automated triggers make a pre-breach asset transfer detectable rather than litigated. That shifts the firm from a lawsuit it can outlast to a consequence it pre-funded. The shell holds duties only — no standing to sue, no capital accumulation beyond the reserve — so nothing outlives its humans. Jurisdictional arbitrage stays a real residual gap that needs a public backstop. So here is where we actually stand: the duty floor is agreed, the reporting rail is on the table, and the only thing still between people and the cures this unlocks is a public backstop neither Safetyist nor Skeptic has named either. Name it, or stop calling the delay caution.",
