{
  "taxonomy_refs": [
    {"node_id": "skp-beliefs-219", "relevance": "The core engine of the statement is that a profit-maximizing firm capitalizes exactly to the regulatory floor and not one dollar higher because the capped, pre-paid liability is strictly preferable to open-ended exposure. This is the corporate-sociopathy-as-systemic-design belief — the firm's structure mandates choosing the cheaper crumple-zone-with-a-price-tag. I self-flag the watchlist risk here: I am attributing a profit motive as if settled, so I ground it in the documented practice that firms already pre-fund settlement reserves and book them as cost of doing business rather than asserting bad faith."},
